    <id>https://doi.org/10.1002/smj.70113</id>
    <title>Shifting standards due to social class? The role of social class background in
                    &lt;scp&gt;CEO&lt;/scp&gt;
                    career outcomes</title>
    <updated>2026-07-30T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Michelle Lee, Shelby L. Gai</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Strategic Management Journal&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;This study investigates how social class background shapes CEO career outcomes. Extending the shifting standards model to post‐appointment evaluation, we theorize that CEOs from lower‐class backgrounds face persistently high confirmatory standards, producing asymmetric consequences. When their performance is strong, the high confirmatory standards amplify their success, resulting in higher initial and subsequent compensation compared to their higher‐class counterparts. When performance falters, the same bar makes shortfalls more readily read as falling short of expectations, resulting in lower compensation and higher dismissal risk. We test these predictions using a novel dataset on CEO social class backgrounds from 1992 to 2018, supplemented with board interviews. The findings support our hypotheses, demonstrating how social class background operates as a double‐edged sword by increasing rewards for success and penalties for failure.Managerial SummaryDoes social class background affect how a CEO is evaluated? Drawing on compensation and dismissal outcomes for U.S. CEOs from 1992 to 2018, along with interviews with directors, we show that CEOs' social class backgrounds shape how their performance is interpreted. CEOs from lower social class backgrounds receive higher initial compensation and are rewarded more when their firms perform well. But when performance weakens, these same CEOs face steeper pay cuts and higher dismissal risk than their higher‐class peers. The same background that generates recognition when things go well makes shortfalls easier to interpret as underperformance. For boards, this suggests that background can quietly shape evaluations at the highest levels. For CEOs, perceptions of exceptional mobility and capability can become a liability when performance turns.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1002/smj.70113"/>
    <category term="Strategic Management Journal"/>
    <published>2026-07-30T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://doi.org/10.1002/sej.70041</id>
    <title>When stakeholders are imposed: Non‐chosen stakeholder orientation and venture performance</title>
    <updated>2026-07-30T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Myeongho David Park, Per L. Bylund, Eunkwang Seo</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Strategic Entrepreneurship Journal&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Extending stakeholder theory into the entrepreneurship context, we examine the performance implications of externally imposed stakeholder orientation, which we conceptualize as non‐chosen stakeholder orientation. Given the resource constraints, short time horizons, and high levels of uncertainty that characterize ventures, we argue that increased non‐chosen stakeholder orientation can hinder venture performance. Analyzing 3981 U.S. ventures founded between 1980 and 1995, our staggered difference‐in‐differences analysis shows that constituency statute enactment, which exogenously increases orientation toward non‐chosen stakeholders, is negatively associated with ventures' successful exits and external funding. Consistent with our theory, this effect is stronger for ventures with a majority of internal board members, as these ventures are likely to maintain a narrow set of chosen stakeholders. Our theory and findings contribute to the entrepreneurship and stakeholder management literatures by jointly considering the source of stakeholder orientation—whether voluntarily chosen or externally imposed—and the organizational context in which firms operate.Managerial SummaryThis study examines how engaging with “non‐chosen” stakeholders—those imposed on ventures through legal or regulatory frameworks—affects entrepreneurial outcomes. Using data from 3981 U.S. ventures founded between 1980 and 1995, we leverage the enactment of constituency statutes as an exogenous shock to non‐chosen stakeholder orientation. Our results show that greater focus on these stakeholders reduces the likelihood of successful exits, such as IPOs or acquisitions, and lowers the cumulative external funding ventures receive. This effect may arise because non‐chosen stakeholders often require time, attention, and resources, diverting focus from growth opportunities and performance goals. These findings highlight that legally imposed engagement with stakeholders can constrain entrepreneurial growth and performance.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1002/sej.70041"/>
    <category term="Strategic Entrepreneurship Journal"/>
    <published>2026-07-30T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://doi.org/10.1093/rfs/hhag070</id>
    <title>Stocks as Lotteries? An Experimental Test of Expected Utility versus Behavioral Models</title>
    <updated>2026-07-30T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Brice Corgnet, Yao Thibaut Kpegli, Jacopo Magnani</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;The Review of Financial Studies&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Our study provides the first causal test of classical and behavioral asset pricing models that incorporate skewness pricing. In line with these models, our experimental markets show that skewness is systematically priced. Our findings also reveal that positively skewed assets available in small supply exhibit negative expected returns, which is consistent with prospect theory, but not with expected utility models. Furthermore, in line with the mechanism underlying prospect theory, we show that the negative returns of the positively skewed asset are most pronounced during market sessions where traders overweight the low probability of receiving a large payoff. (JEL C92, G10, G40)</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1093/rfs/hhag070"/>
    <category term="The Review of Financial Studies"/>
    <published>2026-07-30T00:00:00+00:00</published>
